            or the proposition
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq x \leq b
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_a^b f(x) \,\mathrm{d}x
          %p
            An integral of the type considered here is sometimes called a
            %strong definite integral
            to contrast with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indefinite+integral"} indefinite
            (or semidefinite) integral. Whereas a definite integral is typically a number or some other definite quantity, an indefinite integral is typically another variable quantity of the same type as the integrand (or even more indefinitely, an equivalence class of such quantities). Thus,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_a^b f(x) \,\mathrm{d}x
            is a definite integral,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi a
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_a f(x) \,\mathrm{d}x
            is a semidefinite integral, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int f(x) \,\mathrm{d}x
            is an indefinite integral.
          %p
            %strong Integration
            in the most narrow sense is a process involved in defining or computing integrals.
          %h2#AnalyticIntegration Analytic integration
          %h3#GeneralizingSums Generalizing sums
          %p
            A
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sum"} sum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi S
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{s \in S} a_s
            is defined over a domain
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            which is, as a rule, a
            %em discrete
            set. This set is also typically fixed in the sense that no subdivision is assumed in its definition, except sometimes for convergence purposes. Thus a general sum takes two arguments: a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            and a function on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            and outputs a value. In the case of sum of an infinite series the order may matter as the limiting procedure is taken into account.
          %p
            An
            %strong integral
            is a generalization of a sum where the domain is typically not a discrete set, but some mathematical object, which may be (typically not in a single fixed way, but in many possible ways) approximated or split into pieces. The function to be integrated over the range, is some object which is thought of as living over the range, some sort of a cocycle or a distribution. The basic property from the integral is that it should behave nicely (typically additively in some sense) with respect to combining its values on pieces in the range and that, for a reasonable set of subdivisions the result should be the same (sometimes after passing to a limit).
          %p For a fixed range, integral is typically an operator/functional on a set/space of allowed objects over the range.
          %h3#integration_as_opposite_to_differentiation Integration as opposite to differentiation
          %p
            Many integrals are supposed to be inverse to differentiation procedures of various kinds. Indeed, if integral is a generalization of a sum, then a difference between two partial sums is a value to be added at a step of summation, and its generalization is some sort of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiation"} differentiation
          %h3#solving_differential_equations_and_constraints Solving differential equations and constraints
          %p
            In some cases, one solves a differential equation by reducing it to a relation of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d F = g
            and then
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo =
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F = \int g
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+homotopy+theory"} rational homotopy theory
          %h2#TopologicaIntegration Topological integration
          %p (…)
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration+in+generalized+cohomology"} integration in generalized cohomology
              %p (…)
          %h2#zoo_of_integrals_and_concepts_of_integration Zoo of integrals and concepts of integration
          %p We list a bunch more notions of integration. Should eventually be turned into something more coherent…
          %p
            See also
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+theory"} measure theory
            (and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+space"} measurable space
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+space"} measure space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+integral"} Lebesgue integral
          %p
            Basic kinds of integrals in (super)analysis: the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniell+integral"} Daniell integral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+integral"} Riemann integral
            (possibly
            %span.newWikiWord
              improper
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/improper+Riemann+integral"} ?
            or
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Henstock+integral"} generalised
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+integral"} Lebesgue integral
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Darboux+integral"} Darboux integral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Henstock+integral"} Henstock integral
            (same as the generalised Riemann integral or the improper Lebesgue integral), the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stieltjes+integral"} Stieltjes
            variations of the above, the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berezin+integral"} Berezin integral
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+integrals"} line integrals
          %p
            Special examples of the above include the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Batalin-Vilkovisky+integral"} Batalin-Vilkovisky integral
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kontsevich+integral"} Kontsevich integral
            = succeed ",the" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Selberg+integral"} Selberg integral
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+Selberg+integral"} elliptic Selberg integral
          %p
            Integration is involved in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+transform"} integral transforms
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+transforms+on+sheaves"} integral transforms on sheaves
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stokes+theorem"} Stokes theorem
          %p
            A special topic includes some infinite-dimensional versions including the well-defined
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wiener+integral"} Wiener integral
            and the more problematic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermionic+path+integral"}> fermionic path integral
              ,
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral+as+a+pull-push+transform"}> path integral as a pull-push transform
              \.
          %p
            The basic problem with the path integral comes from the fact that there is no translation-invariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+measure"} Lebesgue measure
            on an infinite-dimensional real vector space with a finite nonzero value on the unit ball.
